WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.259
Today, I want to talk about one of the most
important moments in recent cinema history.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:08.769
Perhaps, you remember it.

00:00:08.769 --> 00:00:12.670
It went like this: "Don’t you know who I
am?

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.769
I’m the juggernaut, bitch!

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:17.289
I’m the juggernaut, bitch!

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:19.810
I’m the juggernaut, bitch!"

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.141
In case you weren't spending hundreds of hours
on the internet in the early aughts, this

00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:29.140
line from the 2006 summer blockbuster, X-Men:
The Last Stand, was cribbed straight from

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.190
an amateur parody video - "I’m the Juggernaut
bitch!”

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.190
"Motherf**ker, I'ma whoop your ass."

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.190
"Silly bitch, your weapons cannot harm me.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.190
Don't you know who the f**k I am?

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:38.559
I'm the juggernaut!"

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:44.859
It inspired a wildly popular meme, which,
against all odds, traveled from the depths

00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:46.800
of the web to the big screen.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.389
X-Men’s reference to Internet culture was
no mere comedic moment.

00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:55.160
It was the birth of an entirely new relationship
between Hollywood and the internet.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.079
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Memeification
of Actors.

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:24.670
And no spoilers ahead...

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:44.250
I guess.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.549
This X-Men thing wasn’t an isolated incident,
either.

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:52.160
Just months earlier, the world’s most prolific
speaker of profanity, Samuel L. Jackson, saw

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.869
his own fame eclipsed by a series of gifs
when his new film, Snakes on a Plane, was

00:01:56.869 --> 00:01:57.869
announced.

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:01.799
It quickly became an online meme-ified sensation.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.299
Most notably, a couple of Internet fans created
an overdubbed R-rated audio trailer for the

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:08.700
film, inspired by Jackson’s role in Pulp
Fiction.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.090
"I did not ask for motherf**king snakes on
my motherf**king plane!"

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.510
Unable to ignore the Internet’s insatiable
demand for good, old-fashioned Samuel L Jackson

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.150
f-bombs, the film’s producers scheduled
reshoots to add new scenes that played up

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.870
the ridiculousness, including the now-famous
line: "I have had it with these motherf**king

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.239
snakes on this motherf**king plane!"

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:33.569
Samuel L. Jackson’s public persona was crucial
to the internet frenzy.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:38.569
The incredibly salient motherf**king mantra
of the film’s viral bubble exploited Jackson’s

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:41.849
firmly established on-screen image as the
potty-mouthed badass.

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:42.849
"Say 'what' again!

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:43.910
Say 'what' again, I dare you!

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.400
I double dare you, motherf**ker.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.390
Say 'what' one more, g*ddamn time!"

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:55.689
Then, to great humorous effect, it placed
said-badass into a delightfully absurd situation:

00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:59.590
the greatest reptilian invasion in the history
of aviation.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:05.079
Fans were explicitly waiting to see how Samuel
L Jackson, the celebrity entity, would respond

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:06.220
to the scenario.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:11.049
They were envisioning some kind of fabricated
combination of the characters played by Jackson

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:12.860
and the man, himself.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.519
This moment represents what we’re going
to call the "memeification of actors."

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.610
While X-Men’s Juggernaut moment, or Snakes
on a Plane, may seem like a bizarre blip on

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:26.590
our collective cultural radar, it’s much
more than that, encapsulating a revolutionary

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:30.719
breakdown in the separation between film and
fan.

00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:35.060
And yet, at the same time, it represents the
next step in a process that’s preoccupied

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.940
Hollywood’s efforts since its earliest days.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:43.110
While the memeification of actors is new,
the processes that lead up to it aren't.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.549
So how did we get to this moment, a time when
Sad Keanu can literally inspire the sad vengeance-seeking

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:52.939
hero of John Wick or when Ryan Gosling is
known as the face of the “Hey Girl” meme

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:55.590
to scores of people who have never seen his
films?

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.580
First, we have to understand something called
serialization, but not in the narrative sense.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.610
So, what is this kind of serialization?

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.799
Most literally, a franchise.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.769
Most metaphorically, according to media scholar
P. David Marshall, you can compare it to a

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:14.650
series of related products, such as a car
or an on-screen performance, “that are stylistically

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.650
linked.”

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.791
Marshall argues that serialization fulfilled
a need of early day Hollywood producers: it

00:04:19.791 --> 00:04:24.520
helped them cement an individual movie star’s
persona into the public consciousness, thus

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.060
setting them apart from the rest of the pack.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.730
In the early Hollywood star system of the
1920s, branding celebrities was an economic

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:35.640
strategy, a way for studios to make their
films stand out in a crowded market.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.060
It was quite literally product differentiation.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.590
In a sea of attractive movie stars, cultivating
demand for individual actors like your resident

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:48.810
‘Cowboy” or “Blonde Bombshell” was
like popularizing Diet Coke to the other studios’

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:54.750
“soda pop.”And so, a complex infrastructure
of public relations professionals, star magazines,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.400
and studios grew in the pursuit of moulding
actors into name brands.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.530
Once a star became a household name, studios
could then exploit said star’s fame by making

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.070
the exact same, generally sh*tty serialized
movies over and over, using their resident

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.780
“good girl,” "Casanova,” or “vamp.”

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.480
When people got bored, actors could be cast
against type, and thus consciously evoke the

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.980
very same audience expectations.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.060
For example, Schwarzenegger became a brand,
so the studios gave you Terminator, Commando,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.500
Predator, The Running Man, etc.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.750
Tired of that? then, they gave you Junior
and Jingle All the Way, where our Last Action

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:36.410
Hero has to fight not with aliens, but with
Sinbad for a toy.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.900
With this serialization of performances, came
what Marshall calls "celebrity personnage.”

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.430
Personnage refers to “a structure of familiarity
for the audience, but also a structure of

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:47.970
performance for the actor.”

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.160
In other words, a feedback loop of typecasting.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:56.330
Sometimes, that familiarity becomes so encompassing
that it literally supplants any sense of the

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.010
actor as an autonomous being.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:02.520
When you picture classic stars like James
Dean, you picture them exactly as they looked

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.330
and behaved on screen, not their real selves.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.840
You could say the same about Samuel L Jackson,
who, for all I know, goes home and soothes

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.620
his children to sleep with the calming refrain,
“go the f**k to sleep.”

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:19.370
Because branded stars equaled easy money,
studios invested in them heavily, and Hollywood

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:24.370
celebrity “personnage” was mediated by
the aforementioned PR reps, star magazines,

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:25.780
agencies and more.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:30.030
They worked in synergy to create and safeguard
celebrity reputations.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.060
Back in these good old days, a celebrity name
was enough to generate reliable box office

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.060
returns.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.300
But that’s all changed.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.730
Today, not even heavyweights like Tom Cruise
or J-Law are totally safe bets.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:45.430
That’s partially because celebrity identity
no longer belongs exclusively to powerful

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.840
movie executives.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:47.840
Why?

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.130
Because the internet.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:54.280
It allows us to actively participate in creating
the narrative of each celebrity, giving us,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:59.670
as scholar Paul Mcdonald writes, “the opportunity
for an interactive construction of star discourse

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.830
that was not possible with previous channels
of mass communication.”

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.310
That’s changed our position in the food
chain of media.

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:10.440
According to Marshall, the audience member
is now a “hybrid of producer and user that

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:16.600
is engaged in the media consumption/production”
or a “prosumer” and this fact is “redefining

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.520
the dynamics of popular culture.”

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:19.910
Which brings us to memes.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.780
The Internet meme is comparatively more egalitarian
than most art forms.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.840
It’s nearly effortless to make one, anyone
with an Internet connection, the know-how

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.260
and a couple of minutes of free time can do
it.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.130
And, unlike literally everything else in the
cultural industry, they’re typically not

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.390
made to generate money, they’re made for
the lulz.

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:41.730
Because memes operate somewhat outside the
realm of economics, they can actually act

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:45.320
as an authentic intervention in our consumption
of culture.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.970
Particularly given the source material: memes
can pull from literally anywhere, since we

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:54.500
have access to nearly the entire history of
American modern visual culture at our fingertips.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:59.880
As a result, we can use memes to evoke powerful,
shared moments of our collective cultural

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:00.880
identity.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.140
And we are doing that literally every day.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:08.150
As a result, so much of our visual exposure
to celebrities is happening via user-generated

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:09.150
culture.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.160
This means a more level playing field.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:16.140
Back in the day, fandom meant passively reading
canned stories in gossip magazines or joining

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:17.690
a celebrity fan club.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:22.610
Today, the fan can actively shape the way
they and their entire social networks view

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.790
individual celebrities via dank memes.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.890
All hail the great prosumer!

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:31.340
But now that we have the means to meme - what
are we doing with it?

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.110
When it comes to celebrities, three major
things.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:39.250
First, some memes are reaffirming celebrity
brands and making them even more powerful.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.209
Consider the Sad Keanu meme.

00:08:41.209 --> 00:08:46.050
The meme was instantly comprehensible because
it exaggerated pre-existing notions of Keaunu

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.170
as vulnerable, his life as tragic.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:54.279
It emerged and spread organically in June
2010, after paparazzi published the now-famous

00:08:54.279 --> 00:08:55.279
photo.

00:08:55.279 --> 00:08:59.470
While the meme began with words superimposed
on the image, Photoshoppers got creative,

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:03.519
and suddenly Sad Keanu was kickin it with
everyone from Stalin, to the Breakfast Club

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:04.519
Brat Pack.

00:09:04.519 --> 00:09:08.569
At one point, Sad Keanu was a more popular
Google search than Oprah.

00:09:08.569 --> 00:09:13.310
Hollywood had a knee-jerk reaction to the
blowup, issuing takedown notices to sadkeanu.com.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.640
RIP.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.730
The sad man himself expressed annoyance about
the meme’s characterization of him — "I

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:22.480
mean, do I wish that I didn't get my picture
taken, and, while I was eating a sandwich

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.160
on the streets of New York?

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.160
Yeah."

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:30.680
But whether Keanu is or isn’t sad, the widespread
sharing of the image has the power to literally

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:33.410
shape our conception of his personnage.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:38.070
Because the image of him looking sad was serialized
widely across the internet, the preconception

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:42.040
of his sadness, confirmed and solidified that
personnage.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:47.019
Until, inevitably, Hollywood found a way to
capitalize on it with: John Wick.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:52.050
The film and its titular role, which feels
tailor made for Keanu, casts him as a sad

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:56.550
action hero, creating a sort of feedback loop
between his memed self and the performances

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:57.870
he gives.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.649
Sometimes memes can also recontextualize the
meaning of a celebrity brand and tap into

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:04.160
a larger, broader cultural sentiment.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.949
That process can be totally subconscious.

00:10:05.949 --> 00:10:12.240
So, let’s go back to Samuel L Jackson, who
as previously discussed, saw his badass “personnage”

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.240
set the Internet ablaze.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:17.930
Now, to be clear, the intertextual commentary
about Samuel L Jackson’s unique brand of

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:20.620
badassery is not unique to the Internet.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.740
You can trace it back at least to the days
of Chappelle Show in 2004 — "No, I can't

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:29.439
stop yellin', cuz that’s how I talk, ya
ain't never seen my movies?"

00:10:29.439 --> 00:10:34.749
Since then, memes have reaffirmed and hyperbolized
his persona, giving the commodified “Samuel

00:10:34.749 --> 00:10:39.040
L Jackson” even more value via increased
recognition and visibility.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.480
But at the same time, there’s an underlying
explanation for why Samuel L Jackson, and

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.960
the particular movie “Snakes on a Plane,”
captured so much Internet attention beyond

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.279
Jackson’s famous potty mouth.

00:10:49.279 --> 00:10:50.810
"Personality goes a long way."

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:55.040
Consider the specific setting: a post-9/11
world, in which Jackson, a beloved figure

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.449
of strength, saves an airplane from attack
the perfect encapsulation of post 9/11 anxiety

00:11:00.449 --> 00:11:02.930
and our collective desire to feel safe.

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:07.790
This performance helped solidify Jackson’s
badass rep, which then became a feedback loop

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:12.709
in which Jackson was repeatedly cast to play
the character of “Samuel L. Jackson the

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:13.709
actor.”

00:11:13.709 --> 00:11:17.449
Most recently, that’s landed Jackson a 9-film
deal with Marvel.

00:11:17.449 --> 00:11:19.269
"Motherf--" "Motherf--"

00:11:19.269 --> 00:11:24.769
Meme-making isn’t just a fantastic procrastination
tool: it’s the paradigm-shifting act of

00:11:24.769 --> 00:11:25.959
a modern prosumer.

00:11:25.959 --> 00:11:31.660
What’s more, our ability to make memes,
i.e. to actively participate in cultural production,

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.910
inherently changes the way we view visual
culture.

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:40.149
In seeing movies and tv shows, in part, as
source material for great memes, we’re developing

00:11:40.149 --> 00:11:42.540
a critical distance from the media we consume.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.139
If you’ve ever made a meme, you’ve probably
experienced that light-bulb moment, when you’re

00:11:46.139 --> 00:11:49.439
watching a Youtube video and thought, “Man,
that would look really dope on loop.”

00:11:49.439 --> 00:11:53.139
In that moment, you were thinking less like
a consumer, passively waiting for the next

00:11:53.139 --> 00:11:57.649
thing to happen on screen, and more like a
prosumer, searching for your next source of

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:00.180
inspiration in your day-to-day media consumption.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:04.410
So, what does this mean for the future of
Hollywood casting, and celebrity persona?

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:05.809
Well, a few things.

00:12:05.809 --> 00:12:09.720
First of all, it means that the cultural currents
of Internet fandom could become increasingly

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:14.309
visible in popular entertainment, such as
the potential Netflix heist movie announced

00:12:14.309 --> 00:12:20.240
in 2017, to be based on this classic 2014
meme of Rihanna and actress Lupita Nyong'o

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:21.990
sitting together at a fashion show.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.089
Now, unfortunately, Danny Devito won’t be
playing Detective Pikachu… yet.

00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:31.180
It also means that actor identities will become,
if anything, increasingly reliant on the Internet

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:32.180
masses.

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:36.309
The rapid serialization unique to the medium
of the meme means it will continue to have

00:12:36.309 --> 00:12:41.959
the power to create some of the key defining
moments of popular celebrity and popular culture,

00:12:41.959 --> 00:12:43.079
sometimes overnight.

00:12:43.079 --> 00:12:47.059
And it will guarantee that Bill Murray will
keep wishing you happy birthday for years

00:12:47.059 --> 00:12:48.059
to come.

00:12:48.059 --> 00:12:49.059
So, what do you think?

00:12:49.059 --> 00:12:53.980
Are memes creating a new class of prosumers
or are they just good for the lulz and an

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:54.980
upvote?

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.529
Let us know what you think in the comments,
and as always, thanks for watching, guys.

00:12:57.529 --> 00:12:57.930
Peace.

